8. Your Monotonic Stack Code

Your approach is the optimal O(n) approach.

However, there is one important error in the final return statement.

You currently have:

return min-max;

It must be:

return max-min;

because the problem asks:

maximum - minimum

Your boundary logic itself is valid.

9. Your Corrected Code
class Solution {
    public long subArrayRanges(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;

        int left[] = new int[n];
        int right[] = new int[n];

        int up[] = new int[n];
        int down[] = new int[n];

        Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();
        Stack<Integer> st1 = new Stack<>();

        // -----------------------------
        // Minimum: Next Smaller
        // -----------------------------
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            while (!st.isEmpty() &&
                   nums[st.peek()] >= nums[i]) {
                st.pop();
            }

            if (!st.isEmpty()) {
                right[i] = st.peek();
            } else {
                right[i] = n;
            }

            st.push(i);
        }

        // -----------------------------
        // Minimum: Previous Smaller or Equal
        // -----------------------------
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            while (!st1.isEmpty() &&
                   nums[st1.peek()] > nums[i]) {
                st1.pop();
            }

            if (!st1.isEmpty()) {
                left[i] = st1.peek();
            } else {
                left[i] = -1;
            }

            st1.push(i);
        }

        st.clear();
        st1.clear();

        // -----------------------------
        // Maximum: Next Greater
        // -----------------------------
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            while (!st.isEmpty() &&
                   nums[st.peek()] <= nums[i]) {
                st.pop();
            }

            if (!st.isEmpty()) {
                down[i] = st.peek();
            } else {
                down[i] = n;
            }

            st.push(i);
        }

        // -----------------------------
        // Maximum: Previous Greater or Equal
        // -----------------------------
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            while (!st1.isEmpty() &&
                   nums[st1.peek()] < nums[i]) {
                st1.pop();
            }

            if (!st1.isEmpty()) {
                up[i] = st1.peek();
            } else {
                up[i] = -1;
            }

            st1.push(i);
        }

        long max = 0;
        long min = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            max += (long) (i - up[i])
                    * (down[i] - i)
                    * nums[i];

            min += (long) (i - left[i])
                    * (right[i] - i)
                    * nums[i];
        }

        return max - min;
    }
}

10. Understanding Your Four Arrays

This is the most important part of your code.

You have:

int left[];
int right[];
int up[];
int down[];

Think of them as:

left  → minimum boundary on LEFT
right → minimum boundary on RIGHT

up    → maximum boundary on LEFT
down  → maximum boundary on RIGHT

More specifically:

Array	Meaning	Stack Type
left[i]	Previous Smaller-or-Equal	Increasing
right[i]	Next Smaller	Increasing
up[i]	Previous Greater-or-Equal	Decreasing
down[i]	Next Greater	Decreasing
11. Why These Comparisons?

Your minimum logic:

// Right
while (!st.isEmpty() && nums[st.peek()] >= nums[i])
    st.pop();

This leaves a strictly smaller element.

Therefore:

right = Next Smaller

Your left logic:

while (!st1.isEmpty() && nums[st1.peek()] > nums[i])
    st1.pop();

This leaves a smaller-or-equal element.

Therefore:

left = Previous Smaller-or-Equal

This asymmetric handling is important for duplicate values.

For maximum:

// Right
while (!st.isEmpty() && nums[st.peek()] <= nums[i])
    st.pop();

leaves:

Next Greater

And:

while (!st1.isEmpty() && nums[st1.peek()] < nums[i])
    st1.pop();

leaves:

Previous Greater-or-Equal
