13. Why Does the Contribution Formula Work?

Suppose:

nums[i] = 5

and its boundaries are:

left[i] = 2
right[i] = 6

So:

       left        i        right
        ↓         ↓          ↓
...  2  ...  ...  5  ...  ...  6 ...

For 5 to remain the minimum:

Starting point

Can start anywhere from:

i

back to:

left[i] + 1

Number of choices:

i - left[i]
Ending point

Can end anywhere from:

i

to:

right[i] - 1

Number of choices:

right[i] - i

Therefore:

total subarrays
=
(i-left[i]) × (right[i]-i)

If nums[i] = 5:

contribution
=
5 × (i-left[i]) × (right[i]-i)

That's the entire mathematical trick.

14. Duplicate Values — The Important Part

Consider:

nums = [2, 2]

There are three subarrays:

[2] → range 0
[2] → range 0
[2,2] → range 0

The problem is deciding which 2 owns [2,2].

If both elements claim the same subarray as their minimum, we'd double-count.

That's why your code uses asymmetric comparisons.

For minimum:

// Previous
nums[stack.peek()] > nums[i]

// Next
nums[stack.peek()] >= nums[i]

This means:

Previous → Smaller or Equal
Next     → Smaller

For maximum:

// Previous
nums[stack.peek()] < nums[i]

// Next
nums[stack.peek()] <= nums[i]

This means:

Previous → Greater or Equal
Next     → Greater

This tie-breaking ensures each subarray gets counted exactly once.

15. One Important Correction to Your Code

Your original final section was:

long max=0;
long min=0;

for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
    max+=(long)(i-left[i])*(right[i]-i)*nums[i];
    min+=(long)(i-up[i])*(down[i]-i)*nums[i];
}

return min-max;

There is a naming mismatch here.

Your arrays mean:

left/right → MINIMUM boundaries
up/down    → MAXIMUM boundaries

Therefore the contributions should be:

min += (long)(i-left[i]) * (right[i]-i) * nums[i];

max += (long)(i-up[i]) * (down[i]-i) * nums[i];

And the final answer must be:

return max - min;

So the corrected final portion is:

long max = 0;
long min = 0;

for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

    max += (long)(i - up[i])
            * (down[i] - i)
            * nums[i];

    min += (long)(i - left[i])
            * (right[i] - i)
            * nums[i];
}

return max - min;
Your boundary calculations are fine; the final subtraction was reversed.
16. Complexity Comparison
Approach	Time	Space	Technique
Brute Force	O(n³)	O(1)	Check every element of every subarray
Better	O(n²)	O(1)	Maintain min/max
Contribution	O(n²) if boundaries searched normally	O(n)	Count contribution
Monotonic Stack	O(n)	O(n)	Find all boundaries efficiently

Why is the stack O(n)?

Although there are while loops, each index is pushed once and popped at most once per stack.

So:

push operations = O(n)
pop operations  = O(n)

Therefore:

O(n) + O(n) = O(n)
